WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.430
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:12.840
LUCIA SUBATIN: Hi there.

00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:15.930
I'm Lucia Subatin, technical
lead for Google Cloud Cortex

00:00:15.930 --> 00:00:18.030
framework, and today,
I'd like to deep dive

00:00:18.030 --> 00:00:21.000
into the technical details
of our Cortex Data Foundation

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:22.650
for SAP.

00:00:22.650 --> 00:00:25.260
From previous videos, you
know that Google Cloud Cortex

00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:27.840
framework delivers not just
the reference architecture

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:30.090
to solve business
problems, but we also

00:00:30.090 --> 00:00:32.189
deliver solution
content, templates,

00:00:32.189 --> 00:00:34.260
and deployment accelerators.

00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:36.570
We provide not just
the recipe, but we also

00:00:36.570 --> 00:00:38.490
include the ingredients
and some pro tips

00:00:38.490 --> 00:00:42.460
to make your meal as
tasty as possible.

00:00:42.460 --> 00:00:45.580
Let's first take a look at the
ingredients for the Cortex Data

00:00:45.580 --> 00:00:47.590
Foundation for SAP.

00:00:47.590 --> 00:00:51.280
We start with raw table data
from an SAP, CC, or S/4HANA

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:52.210
system.

00:00:52.210 --> 00:00:54.190
We want to learn the
data into BigQuery,

00:00:54.190 --> 00:00:55.690
and we want to make
sure the data is

00:00:55.690 --> 00:00:58.390
both consistent and
updated as frequently

00:00:58.390 --> 00:01:00.430
as our business requires.

00:01:00.430 --> 00:01:03.190
The records keep changing
as updates, deletes,

00:01:03.190 --> 00:01:05.440
and inserts flow into BigQuery.

00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:07.300
This means we need
an automated way

00:01:07.300 --> 00:01:10.420
to process these records
that are constantly changing,

00:01:10.420 --> 00:01:13.540
such as scripts to process
Change Data Capture, or CDC

00:01:13.540 --> 00:01:14.860
for short.

00:01:14.860 --> 00:01:17.230
Finally, we need to
make sure our users can

00:01:17.230 --> 00:01:19.930
make sense of the current data,
answer business questions,

00:01:19.930 --> 00:01:21.730
and improve decisions
with insights

00:01:21.730 --> 00:01:24.010
powered by machine learning.

00:01:24.010 --> 00:01:27.430
We also want to give users the
flexibility to consume insights

00:01:27.430 --> 00:01:30.670
with their preferred reporting
tool of choice, an application

00:01:30.670 --> 00:01:33.100
or even a spreadsheet.

00:01:33.100 --> 00:01:35.470
So how does Google
Cloud Cortex framework

00:01:35.470 --> 00:01:37.870
help you put these
ingredients together?

00:01:37.870 --> 00:01:40.840
Let's take a deeper look.

00:01:40.840 --> 00:01:43.660
We want to keep the
integrations options open,

00:01:43.660 --> 00:01:46.750
so any tool that gets the
data into the same structure

00:01:46.750 --> 00:01:52.180
with the same field names as in
the original SAP tables work.

00:01:52.180 --> 00:01:55.720
If you follow an ELT pattern,
as is preferable with BigQuery,

00:01:55.720 --> 00:01:57.400
you need a way to
distinguish which

00:01:57.400 --> 00:02:00.820
is the latest replicated
record for reporting purposes.

00:02:00.820 --> 00:02:04.420
This is accomplished by loading
each record with a timestamp

00:02:04.420 --> 00:02:07.670
and an operation flag.

00:02:07.670 --> 00:02:10.070
Once the raw data
records are in BigQuery,

00:02:10.070 --> 00:02:12.320
you have two options
to process the data,

00:02:12.320 --> 00:02:14.870
leveraging the provided
CDC process in scripts.

00:02:14.870 --> 00:02:17.086
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:02:21.400 --> 00:02:24.880
The first one-- you can generate
views to scan the raw table

00:02:24.880 --> 00:02:27.980
and pick the latest
record in real time.

00:02:27.980 --> 00:02:29.770
This is useful when
you're replicating

00:02:29.770 --> 00:02:32.740
data in near real time from
a tool like our BigQuery

00:02:32.740 --> 00:02:35.290
connector for SAP
SLT and the business

00:02:35.290 --> 00:02:38.120
wants updates reflected
as they happen.

00:02:38.120 --> 00:02:41.230
The second option is to
use scheduled jobs in Cloud

00:02:41.230 --> 00:02:45.190
Composer to merge or upsert
the new or updated record

00:02:45.190 --> 00:02:47.515
in a table in a specific
frequency in batch.

00:02:47.515 --> 00:02:49.762
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:02:55.740 --> 00:02:58.200
This is a good option to
keep costs under control

00:02:58.200 --> 00:03:00.060
when the business does
not plan on querying

00:03:00.060 --> 00:03:01.230
the data in real time.

00:03:01.230 --> 00:03:03.595
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:03:09.280 --> 00:03:12.010
Some replication tools
have embedded change data

00:03:12.010 --> 00:03:15.430
capture processing capability
and merge the records directly

00:03:15.430 --> 00:03:17.330
when inserting them.

00:03:17.330 --> 00:03:20.590
In this case, you can skip the
CDC processing in BigQuery,

00:03:20.590 --> 00:03:24.120
but we supply the
scripts just in case.

00:03:24.120 --> 00:03:26.340
Now that we have processed
our replicated data

00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:28.380
and have the latest
changes in BigQuery,

00:03:28.380 --> 00:03:30.720
it's time to create some models.

00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:34.380
Our Cortex Data Foundation has
more than 60 pre-built views

00:03:34.380 --> 00:03:37.500
that anyone can deploy
and adjust to their needs,

00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:39.510
and the list of modeling
objects available

00:03:39.510 --> 00:03:42.840
will continue to grow as
we release new content.

00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:44.550
You will find the
current list of use

00:03:44.550 --> 00:03:45.925
on the tables
they're referencing

00:03:45.925 --> 00:03:48.090
in the entity relationship
diagram in the GitHub

00:03:48.090 --> 00:03:51.030
repository link below.

00:03:51.030 --> 00:03:54.690
These views not only incorporate
SAP specific business modeling,

00:03:54.690 --> 00:03:57.480
but they also tackle
common but technically hard

00:03:57.480 --> 00:04:00.630
challenges to solve, such as
unit of measure and currency

00:04:00.630 --> 00:04:04.680
conversions, and the ability
to deploy SCC and S/4 models

00:04:04.680 --> 00:04:07.590
separately are consolidated
into a single view.

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:13.260
You will also find scripts
to process data in batches,

00:04:13.260 --> 00:04:15.690
flatten hierarchies,
and consume and store

00:04:15.690 --> 00:04:19.200
data from other sources, such
as weather or search trends,

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:20.894
through directed acyclic graphs.

00:04:20.894 --> 00:04:22.706
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:04:26.200 --> 00:04:29.080
These scripts are ready to
be executed by Cloud Composer

00:04:29.080 --> 00:04:31.360
within a schedule you
can customize according

00:04:31.360 --> 00:04:34.640
to your business needs.

00:04:34.640 --> 00:04:36.640
Everything you need to
know about the deployment

00:04:36.640 --> 00:04:39.970
of our Cortex Data Foundation
is documented in our open source

00:04:39.970 --> 00:04:42.700
GitHub repository.

00:04:42.700 --> 00:04:44.500
You will find the
key starting point

00:04:44.500 --> 00:04:47.530
is the Cloud Build file that
executes all of the steps,

00:04:47.530 --> 00:04:52.060
from optionally providing test
data through generating change

00:04:52.060 --> 00:04:55.600
data capture scripts to updates
tables with the latest results,

00:04:55.600 --> 00:05:00.320
to creating the views that
provide the business logics.

00:05:00.320 --> 00:05:02.690
After you have cloned
your repository,

00:05:02.690 --> 00:05:05.930
you can customize parameters
to match your business needs

00:05:05.930 --> 00:05:07.370
and available data sources.

00:05:10.400 --> 00:05:12.780
And once your Cloud Build
deployment is complete,

00:05:12.780 --> 00:05:15.110
you will see the data sets
you used as parameters

00:05:15.110 --> 00:05:16.400
have been populated.

00:05:19.910 --> 00:05:22.250
And you will find scripts
to process changes

00:05:22.250 --> 00:05:24.380
and retrieve additional
data in the bucket

00:05:24.380 --> 00:05:27.110
you have provisioned.

00:05:27.110 --> 00:05:30.680
These models are foundation
to more advanced solutions.

00:05:30.680 --> 00:05:33.080
For example, on the
Google Cloud marketplace,

00:05:33.080 --> 00:05:35.840
you will find our Cortex
Application layer content

00:05:35.840 --> 00:05:39.050
with sample applications for
synchronously exposing data

00:05:39.050 --> 00:05:42.140
through REST APIs or integrating
with other applications

00:05:42.140 --> 00:05:44.450
through pops up.

00:05:44.450 --> 00:05:47.180
Now that you know what Cortex
Data Foundation is about,

00:05:47.180 --> 00:05:49.310
check out the GitHub
repository link here

00:05:49.310 --> 00:05:52.430
to deploy the data
foundation solution content.

00:05:52.430 --> 00:05:55.800
You will find all the step
by step instructions there.

00:05:55.800 --> 00:05:57.260
And this is just the beginning.

